@@s008 What machine learning is
@@s009 The first lecture in six ideas: instead of writing rules, you let the computer learn them from the data. Here you find the difference from traditional programming, the pipeline to memorise, the types of problems, why data alone is not enough, and the recipe that the course repeats for 14 weeks.
@@s010 Programming or learning from data
@@s011 A <b>traditional programme</b> is a list of rules written by a person. To recognise a cat in a photo you would have to write, by hand, what ears, whiskers and fur look like: in practice impossible, because cats are all different and photos change with the light.
@@s012 In <b>machine learning</b> the work is reversed. You do not write the rules: you collect many <b>examples</b> whose answer you already know (this photo: cat; this one: not a cat) and let the computer find a rule that explains them. The rule found is called a <b>model</b> (<i>model</i>): it is, in every respect, a programme, only nobody wrote it line by line.
@@s013 Two consequences that come back throughout the course. First: the model is only as good as the data it has seen; if the examples are few or wrong, the rule will be wrong. Second: the model must always be <b>checked</b> on new examples, because a rule that works only on the examples already seen is useless.
@@s014 Two ways of getting a programme
@@s015 The pipeline: from raw data to prediction
@@s016 Slide 21 literally says "memorize this": it is the scheme of every ML project, and every week of the course explores one piece of it. There are two phases.
@@s017 In the <b>training</b> phase the raw data (<i>raw data</i>) are cleaned (<i>data wrangling</i>: errors and noise are removed, the scales are normalised), then transformed into <b>feature vectors</b> (<i>feature vectors</i>): each example becomes a list of numbers. The model receives these vectors together with the <b>labels</b> (<i>labels</i>, the right answers) and adjusts its <b>parameters</b> so as to get as few things wrong as possible.
@@s018 In the <b>prediction</b> phase new data arrive. They go through the <b>same</b> transformations as in training, enter the already trained model, and the prediction comes out. Finally the <b>evaluation</b> (<i>evaluation</i>) compares the predictions with the truth, on data the model has not used to learn, with a metric chosen in advance (for example sensitivity and specificity in the medical example of slide 6).
@@s019 The questions that the slides mark as likely at the exam all come from here: what a model is and how models differ, how a good model is chosen, how the parameters are found.
@@s020 The machine learning pipeline (slides 20–21)
@@s021 Supervised and unsupervised
@@s022 A <b>dataset</b> is a table: one row per example, one column for each measured feature (in slide 17: height, weight, shoe size, gender). Each row becomes a vector.
@@s023 In <b>supervised learning</b> (<i>supervised learning</i>) the dataset also has a column with the <b>answer</b> we want to predict (in the slide: "Ballet?"). The model learns the relation between the input columns and the answer. If the answer is a <b>category</b> (yes/no, cat/dog) the problem is called <b>classification</b>; if it is a <b>number</b> (a price, a gaze position) it is called <b>regression</b>.
@@s024 In <b>unsupervised learning</b> (<i>unsupervised learning</i>) the column of answers is not there: "no predefined and known set of outcomes". The model looks for hidden structures, for example groups of similar examples (<b>clustering</b>, week 13) or directions in which the data vary most (PCA, week 12).
@@s025 The same points, three types of problem
@@s026 True function, data and noise
@@s027 The basic idea of ML: there exists a <b>true function</b> (<i>ground truth</i>) that links the input x to the output y, for example the pupil position and the point looked at on the screen. We do not know it. We can only <b>collect data</b>: measured pairs (x, y).
@@s028 Each measurement contains a small random error, the <b>noise</b> (<i>noise</i>): the instrument is not perfect, the person moves. That is why the points do not lie exactly on the true function.
@@s029 The model is our attempt to reconstruct the true function starting only from the points. Its aim is not to pass through the points already seen, but to <b>predict new points well</b> (<i>unseen data</i>). A model flexible enough to pass through all the points also copies the noise and gets it badly wrong elsewhere: it is the problem of <i>overfitting</i>, which returns in weeks 6–7.
@@s030 To choose, you must make a <b>hypothesis</b> about the shape of the function (is it a line? is it smooth?). This hypothesis is called <b>inductive bias</b> (<i>inductive bias</i>): without it, from a finite number of points nothing can be learned.
@@s031 The hidden target: a line against a polynomial of degree 6
@@s032 Training and test: machine learning in brief
@@s033 Slide 25 sums it all up in two sentences. <b>Training</b>: given a set of labelled examples {(x₁, y₁), …, (x_N, y_N)}, one estimates the prediction function f by making the error on the examples as small as possible. <b>Test</b>: f is applied to an example x never seen, and the predicted value ŷ = f(x) is returned.
@@s034 The symbols, one by one. <b>x</b> is the input, usually a feature vector. <b>y</b> is the right output. The subscript <b>i</b> in xᵢ numbers the examples, from 1 to <b>N</b>. <b>f</b> is the model. <b>ŷ</b> ("y hat") is the <b>predicted</b> output: the hat distinguishes the prediction from the measured value.
@@s035 The function f depends on <b>parameters</b> (<i>model parameters</i>, often called <i>weights</i> w): for a line they are the intercept w₀ and the slope w₁. Learning means finding the values of the parameters; once they are fixed, the model is an ordinary function.
@@s036 From the training set to the prediction
@@s037 The recipe: model, loss, parameters
@@s038 Slide 33 gives the recipe that the course applies to every method. <b>1</b>: you have training data. <b>2</b>: you choose two things, the <b>model</b> (the family of functions, for example "all the lines") and the <b>loss function</b> (<i>loss</i> or <i>objective function</i>), that is the number that measures how much the model gets wrong. <b>3</b>: the goal is to make the loss as small as possible. <b>4</b>: you <b>learn the parameters</b>, that is you look for the values that reach that minimum.
@@s039 The two functions have different roles (slide 29). The <b>prediction function</b> contains our hypotheses about how the problem works; the parameters are its "brain". The <b>loss function</b> says how much it costs to get it wrong; changing the parameters tries to make it go down.
@@s040 In the animation the parameters are tried by hand, as in the first slides ("manually selecting model parameters"). The method to find the minimum directly comes in week 5 (least squares) and in week 14 (gradient descent). The loss used here is explained symbol by symbol in <a href="w01-loss-minimi-quadrati.html">least squares loss</a>.
@@s041 The recipe in action: four lines, four losses
